Q: How do I implement ToString for my custom primitive type that does not need a Formatter without going against Rust&#39;s guidelines? A: <p>Although the formatting code would ideally be zero-cost (Rust has all the information to elide it at run-time), in practice <a href="https://blog.m-ou.se/format-args/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">things are more messy</a> and some optimization opportunities are lost. This is why the standard library chose to use experimental compiler features (specialization) to implement this efficiently. But sadly those features are very unlikely to be stabilized in the near future, so you cannot use them in your own code.</p>
<p>Your worry that using <code>Display</code> will be less efficient is not completely misplaced. If we take this code:</p>
<pre><code>use std::fmt::{Display, Formatter};

pub struct MyBool1(bool);

impl Display for MyBool1 {
    #[inline(always)]
    fn fmt(&amp;self, f: &amp;mut Formatter&lt;'_&gt;) -&gt; Result&lt;(), std::fmt::Error&gt; {
        write!(f, &quot;{}&quot;, if self.0 { &quot;true&quot; } else { &quot;false&quot; })
    }
}

pub fn print_via_formatter(v: MyBool1) -&gt; String {
    v.to_string()
}

pub struct MyBool2(bool);

impl ToString for MyBool2 {
    #[inline(always)]
    fn to_string(&amp;self) -&gt; String {
        (if self.0 { &quot;true&quot; } else { &quot;false&quot; }).to_string()
    }
}

pub fn print_via_to_string(v: MyBool2) -&gt; String {
    v.to_string()
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/c8Kqzqrrf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Godbolt shows</a> that <code>print_via_formatter()</code> is indeed calling into the formatting machinery, while <code>print_via_to_string()</code> only calls into the allocator. A criterion benchmark on my laptop shows that the version that implements <code>ToString</code> directly is approximately 2.6x faster, taking 8ns compared to 21ns for the version that gets <code>ToString</code> via <code>Display</code>.</p>
<p>Having said that, note that the difference is in literal nanoseconds, and is unlikely to matter in real-life code. Unless you are writing some sort of high-performance code, or are creating a library which might be used in such code, you probably shouldn't care about this difference - and if you are, you probably shouldn't be using <code>to_string()</code> to begin with. The standard library is right to optimize <code>to_string()</code> because it's often used as a simple way of getting an owned string from a <code>&amp;str</code>, which would be a problem if it were significantly slower than <code>to_owned()</code>. (This was in fact the case in early Rust versions, and new users were warned to prefer <code>to_owned()</code> to the more logically named <code>to_string()</code>.)</p>

